            </m:annotation-xml>
          </m:semantics>
        </m:math>
    </para>
      <para id="para-00104">Notice that, in the case when \(m\) and \(n\) have opposite signs, the sign of the one with the larger absolute
  value matches the sign of \(b\).</para>
    </note>
    <note class="try" id="note-00003">
<title>Try It: How to Factor a Trinomial of the Form \(x^2 + bx + c\)</title>
    
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00105">Factor each trinomial.</para>
          <para id="para-00106">\(
      q^2 + 10q + 24\)</para>
          <para id="para-00107">\(−7n + 12 + n^2\)</para>
          <para id="para-00108">\(m^2 − 13mn + 12n^2\)</para>
          <para id="para-00109">Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00110">Check your solutions for factoring trinomials of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\).</para>
          <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00003">
            <item>\(
    (q + 4)(q + 6)\)</item>
          </list>
          <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00004">
            <item>\((n − 3)(n − 4)\)</item>
          </list>
          <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00005">
            <item>\((m − n)(m − 12n)\)</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </note>
  </section>
</section>

